        <!-- Pull Quote -->
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic border-l-4 border-[#38BDF8] pl-6 bg-gray-50 py-4 rounded-r-xl">
          The single biggest cost variable in any garage floor epoxy project isn't the product — it's whether you do it yourself or hire a professional. That one decision swings the price from $150 to over $8,000 for the exact same floor.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <!-- SECTION 1: Cost at a Glance -->
        <div id="cost-at-a-glance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Epoxy Garage Floor Cost at a Glance
          </h2>
          <p>
            Before diving into the detailed breakdowns, here is the big picture. The table below shows realistic total-project costs across the most common garage sizes and coating approaches. These figures include materials for DIY projects and both materials and labor for professional installations. They do not include crack repair or moisture remediation, which are covered in the hidden costs section below.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
            <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-900 text-white">
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-sm">Approach</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-sm">One-Car (~250 sq ft)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-sm">Two-Car (~500 sq ft)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-sm">Large (~750 sq ft)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-900">DIY Water-Based Kit</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$120–$180</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$220–$350</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$320–$500</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-900">DIY High-Solids (70%)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$200–$300</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$380–$550</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$550–$800</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-900">DIY 100% Solids</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$300–$500</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$550–$900</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$800–$1,300</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-900">Professional Basic</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$750–$1,250</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$1,500–$2,500</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$2,250–$3,750</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-900">Professional Premium</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$1,250–$2,000</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$2,500–$4,000</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$3,750–$6,000</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-900">Full Polyaspartic System</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$2,000–$3,000</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$4,000–$6,000</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$6,000–$9,000</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            A few things jump out immediately. First, the spread within each category is wide — a professional basic install on a two-car garage might cost $1,500 or $2,500 depending on surface condition, regional labor rates, and the specific contractor. Second, a properly executed DIY 100% solids system on a large garage costs roughly the same as a professional basic install on a one-car garage. Third, the full polyaspartic professional system costs 15 to 25 times as much as a DIY water-based kit covering the same floor. Whether that premium is justified depends entirely on your use case, which the cost-per-year section below addresses.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 2: DIY Cost Breakdown -->
        <div id="cost-calculator" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. DIY Cost Breakdown: What You're Actually Buying
          </h2>
          <p>
            When you see a garage floor epoxy kit priced at $120, that number is deceptive — it's the cost of the epoxy itself, not the full project. A complete DIY epoxy project requires several additional products and supplies that most first-timers don't account for. Here is an honest itemized breakdown for a typical two-car garage (approximately 500 square feet), priced at current retail rates as of spring 2026.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-6">Full DIY Project Cost — Two-Car Garage (~500 sq ft)</p>
            <div class="space-y-4">
              <div class="flex justify-between items-start border-b border-white/10 pb-4">
                <div>
                  <p class="font-bold text-white">Epoxy Kit (water-based, two-part)</p>
                  <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm">1–2 kits at 250–500 sq ft coverage each</p>
                </div>
                <span class="text-[#38BDF8] font-black whitespace-nowrap ml-4">$120–$220</span>
              </div>
              <div class="flex justify-between items-start border-b border-white/10 pb-4">
                <div>
                  <p class="font-bold text-white">Concrete Etching Acid / Etch Solution</p>
                  <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm">Often included in kit, otherwise $20–$30 per gallon</p>
                </div>
                <span class="text-[#38BDF8] font-black whitespace-nowrap ml-4">$0–$30</span>
              </div>
              <div class="flex justify-between items-start border-b border-white/10 pb-4">
                <div>
                  <p class="font-bold text-white">Concrete Degreaser</p>
                  <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm">TSP substitute or Purple Power concentrate</p>
                </div>
                <span class="text-[#38BDF8] font-black whitespace-nowrap ml-4">$15–$25</span>
              </div>
              <div class="flex justify-between items-start border-b border-white/10 pb-4">
                <div>
                  <p class="font-bold text-white">Rollers, Roller Frames, Extension Pole</p>
                  <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm">3/8-inch nap rollers (need 2–3), frame, 4-foot extension</p>
                </div>
                <span class="text-[#38BDF8] font-black whitespace-nowrap ml-4">$25–$45</span>
              </div>
              <div class="flex justify-between items-start border-b border-white/10 pb-4">
                <div>
                  <p class="font-bold text-white">Decorative Color Flake Chips</p>
                  <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm">If not included in kit; 1 lb per 100 sq ft for medium coverage</p>
                </div>
                <span class="text-[#38BDF8] font-black whitespace-nowrap ml-4">$0–$50</span>
              </div>
              <div class="flex justify-between items-start border-b border-white/10 pb-4">
                <div>
                  <p class="font-bold text-white">Clear Polyurethane or Polyaspartic Topcoat</p>
                  <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm">Strongly recommended; 1 gallon covers 400–500 sq ft</p>
                </div>
                <span class="text-[#38BDF8] font-black whitespace-nowrap ml-4">$40–$90</span>
              </div>
              <div class="flex justify-between items-start border-b border-white/10 pb-4">
                <div>
                  <p class="font-bold text-white">Stiff-Bristle Scrub Brush or Floor Brush</p>
                  <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm">For working degreaser into oil-contaminated areas</p>
                </div>
                <span class="text-[#38BDF8] font-black whitespace-nowrap ml-4">$10–$20</span>
              </div>
              <div class="flex justify-between items-start border-b border-white/10 pb-4">
                <div>
                  <p class="font-bold text-white">Safety Equipment (gloves, eye protection, respirator)</p>
                  <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm">Required for acid etching and solvent-based products</p>
                </div>
                <span class="text-[#38BDF8] font-black whitespace-nowrap ml-4">$15–$40</span>
              </div>
              <div class="flex justify-between items-start">
                <div>
                  <p class="font-bold text-white">Painter's Tape for Wall Edges</p>
                  <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm">To protect baseboards and walls during application</p>
                </div>
                <span class="text-[#38BDF8] font-black whitespace-nowrap ml-4">$10–$15</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="mt-6 pt-6 border-t border-white/20 flex justify-between items-center">
              <p class="text-xl font-black text-white">Total DIY Project Cost (Two-Car Garage)</p>
              <span class="text-2xl font-black text-[#FF4500]">$235–$535</span>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The wide range reflects real purchasing decisions. If you buy individual components (degreaser, etch, rollers) and a premium high-solids kit with a separate topcoat, you'll approach the upper end. If you buy a single all-inclusive kit that bundles the etching solution, chips, and a topcoat, and you already have basic tools, the lower end is achievable.
          </p>
          <p>
            One cost many DIYers don't plan for is concrete crack repair. If your floor has cracks wider than a hairline — which most garages over 10 years old do — you'll need either an epoxy crack filler ($15–$30 per tube) or a hydraulic cement compound ($20–$40) before applying the floor coating. Budget an additional $30–$100 for crack repair on an average older garage floor. Attempting to bridge cracks with epoxy alone results in the coating reopening along the same crack lines within months.
          </p>
          <p>
            For a one-car garage (250 square feet), divide the above figures roughly in half: expect $130–$280 for a complete DIY project with a water-based kit. For a large three-car garage around 750 square feet, multiply by 1.5 and plan for $350–$800 total depending on the product system you choose.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 3: Professional Cost -->
        <div id="professional-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Professional Installation Cost: What Drives the Price?
          </h2>
          <p>
            Professional garage floor coating prices are not arbitrary. When you hire a contractor, you're paying for diamond-grinding equipment that costs $3,000–$8,000 to own, commercial-grade coatings priced at $80–$150 per gallon (not the $40 consumer version), 10–16 man-hours of labor, and in most cases a multi-year warranty. Here is an honest breakdown of the specific factors that push professional prices up or down.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Surface Condition Premium</h3>
          <p>
            Surface preparation is where professional floor contractors make or lose money. A clean, sound concrete slab in good condition takes roughly 4–6 hours to diamond-grind and prepare before coating can begin. A heavily stained floor with oil penetration, previous coating failures, or surface spalling can take twice as long. Most professional contractors add a $200–$600 surface condition premium for floors requiring extensive remediation. Ask your contractor explicitly whether their quote assumes good surface condition or has been adjusted for your specific floor.
          </p>
          <p>
            Previously coated floors are the most expensive to prepare. If your concrete has an existing epoxy, paint, or sealer layer that needs to be removed, expect to pay $0.50–$1.50 per square foot in additional prep labor. On a two-car garage, that adds $250–$750 to the base quote. Some contractors price this as a separate line item; others bundle it at a higher per-square-foot rate. Either way, a floor with an existing coating almost always costs more to refinish than bare concrete.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Coating System Chosen</h3>
          <p>
            This is the biggest single cost variable within professional quotes. There is an enormous difference in price and performance between a basic single-coat water-based epoxy system and a three-coat polyaspartic system. Most professional contractors offer at least two tiers; the better ones offer three or four. The coating system drives both the materials cost and the labor time, since higher-tier systems require more coats with cure time between each.
          </p>
          <p>
            A basic water-based epoxy system — one coat of epoxy, broadcast chips, no topcoat — is the cheapest professional option. It costs $3–$5 per square foot installed and delivers a floor that should last 5–8 years with normal use. This is what most low-bid contractors quote because the materials are cheap and the application time is fast. It's adequate for utility garages where appearance isn't a priority. A mid-tier premium epoxy system with a urethane topcoat runs $5–$8 per square foot and delivers meaningfully better scratch resistance and longevity. The full three-coat polyaspartic system — diamond-grind prep, 100% solids epoxy base coat, chip broadcast, polyaspartic topcoat — runs $8–$12 per square foot and represents the gold standard for residential garage floor coatings.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <table class="w-full text-left">
              <thead class="bg-gray-900 text-white">
                <tr>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-sm">Professional System</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-sm">Per Sq Ft</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-sm">500 Sq Ft (Two-Car)</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-sm">Expected Lifespan</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-900">Basic (water-based epoxy, chips)</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$3–$5</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$1,500–$2,500</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5–8 years</td>
                </tr>
