          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Time Cost: What DIY Actually Takes</h3>
          <p>
            The $800–$3,500 savings doesn't come free. A proper DIY garage floor epoxy project requires two days of real work. Here is an honest time estimate for someone doing this for the first time on a two-car garage.
          </p>
          <p>
            Preparation day runs approximately 7–8 hours for a thorough job: clear the garage and move all items (1–2 hours), degrease with scrubbing and double rinse (1.5–2 hours), acid etch with thorough scrubbing (1 hour), rinse completely and begin drying (1 hour), patch cracks and surface defects (0.5–1 hour), final inspection and tape perimeter (1 hour). Application day runs approximately 5–6 hours: final sweep and tack (30 minutes), mix and apply epoxy base coat (2–2.5 hours), broadcast decorative chips (included in above), allow initial cure and apply topcoat (1.5–2 hours), cleanup (30–45 minutes). The total is roughly 12–14 hours across two days, which works out to a savings rate of $65–$250 per hour depending on your market. For most people, that is a compelling return on their time.
