        <!-- SECTION 5: Decision Framework -->
        <div id="decision-framework" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What Determines Which Option Is Right for You?
          </h2>
          <p>
            The DIY versus professional decision isn't only about money. Several factors beyond pure cost should shape your choice. Working through these honestly will save you from making a decision you regret in either direction.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Choose DIY If:</h3>
          <p>
            You have a structurally sound concrete floor in reasonable condition — no active cracking, no moisture vapor transmission, no prior coating failures. You have at least one weekend free and you are comfortable following multi-step instructions carefully. You are willing to do thorough prep work, including acid etching, even though it is time-consuming. Your garage is a functional space rather than a showroom or business environment. You want to save $1,500–$3,000 and are willing to invest 12–14 hours of physical work to realize that savings.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Choose Professional Installation If:</h3>
          <p>
            Your floor has active moisture vapor transmission — professional contractors can apply specialized vapor barrier primers that are not available to consumers and that address the underlying issue rather than just masking it. Your floor has had two or more previous coating failures, which suggests a substrate problem that requires professional diagnosis. You want a full polyaspartic system with a 10–15 year warranty — these systems require spray application equipment and multi-coat scheduling that is genuinely beyond DIY capability. Your garage is a commercial space, showroom, or area where a coating failure would be costly and disruptive. You simply don't have the time, physical capacity, or inclination to spend a weekend on intensive physical labor.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-6">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-amber-700 mb-2">The Honest Middle Ground</p>
            <p class="text-amber-900">If you are uncertain, consider a hybrid approach: hire a professional to diamond-grind and prepare the surface (this is the step most homeowners genuinely can't replicate well), then apply a high-quality DIY topcoat yourself. Some contractors will offer preparation-only quotes. You get the critical step done correctly while still saving substantially on materials and finish coating labor.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Hidden Costs -->
        <div id="hidden-costs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Hidden Costs to Budget For
          </h2>
          <p>
            The line items below don't appear in most DIY kit descriptions or contractor quotes unless you specifically ask. Budget for them or be prepared to encounter unplanned expenses mid-project.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Crack Repair</h3>
          <p>
            Most concrete floors have some degree of cracking, particularly at control joints and along walls. Hairline cracks can be bridged by a properly thick epoxy application, but cracks wider than 1/16 inch need to be filled before coating. Epoxy crack filler runs $15–$30 per tube, with a typical garage requiring 2–5 tubes depending on crack severity. For floors with significant structural cracking, polyurethane caulk injected under pressure is the professional solution at $200–$400 additional cost. Factor in at least $30–$100 for crack repair on any garage floor more than 10 years old.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Moisture Vapor Barrier Treatment</h3>
          <p>
            If your floor tests positive for moisture vapor transmission — water droplets under the plastic sheet test — standard epoxy will eventually fail due to hydrostatic pressure lifting the coating from below. The solution is a moisture-tolerant epoxy primer or a dedicated vapor barrier coating applied before the finish coat. These products run $40–$80 per gallon for DIY versions, and professional moisture remediation treatments add $300–$800 to a professional quote. This is not optional if your floor tests positive; applying standard epoxy over a floor with moisture vapor issues guarantees delamination.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Separate Topcoat</h3>
          <p>
            Most consumer epoxy kits do not include a topcoat. The kit contains an epoxy base coat and optional chips, but epoxy alone is not the most durable top surface — it scratches and yellows from UV over time. A polyurethane or polyaspartic topcoat costs $40–$90 per gallon and is applied over the cured epoxy base coat to provide scratch resistance, UV stability, and a more durable surface finish. Budget for it upfront rather than discovering after your first winter that the epoxy surface is looking dull and scratched.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Decorative Chips Extra</h3>
          <p>
            Many epoxy kits advertise the chip broadcast system prominently but include only a partial bag of chips — enough for light coverage but not for the full-broadcast look shown in the marketing photos. If you want 70–100% chip coverage (the dense, fully covered look), budget an additional $20–$50 for supplemental chips. This is a minor cost but one that catches many DIYers by surprise mid-project when they run out of chips halfway through the floor.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Floor Drain Modifications</h3>
          <p>
            Garage floors with center drains require temporary plugging during coating to prevent epoxy from entering the drain, and the drain housing may need to be masked or temporarily raised to stay at finished-floor level after the coating is applied. Most DIYers handle this with painter's tape and plastic sheeting (essentially free). However, if your drain needs to be raised to match the new coating thickness — particularly with thick commercial coatings — a plumber or contractor modification can add $100–$400 to the project. This situation is most common with full polyaspartic systems where the coating thickness is 40–80 mils and visibly raises the floor level relative to the drain housing.
          </p>
        </div>

