        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
            Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Why do professionals charge so much for epoxy garage floors?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Professional garage floor coating prices reflect several real costs that consumer quotes obscure. Diamond grinding equipment costs $3,000–$8,000 to own and operates at significant wear-cost per use. Commercial-grade coatings cost $80–$150 per gallon wholesale versus the $40–$60 you pay at a hardware store. Labor on a two-car garage runs 10–16 man-hours when prep is included. Most professional quotes also include a multi-year warranty against delamination, which means the contractor is bearing the risk of any prep or application failures. The markup over DIY material cost is significant, but it's not arbitrary — it reflects genuine labor, equipment, commercial materials, and warranty costs.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Is DIY epoxy as good as professional installation?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700">With proper surface preparation, a high-quality DIY kit — 70% solids or 100% solids with a topcoat — delivers results that are genuinely comparable to entry-level professional installs. Where the gap is most significant is in surface preparation: professional diamond grinders achieve a surface profile and cleanliness that is very difficult to replicate with DIY acid etching alone. For most homeowners on sound concrete in good condition, a well-prepped DIY project will last 7–12 years and be visually indistinguishable from a basic professional install. The professional premium buys certainty of prep quality, commercial-grade materials, and warranty backing — not necessarily a different product outcome for a typical floor.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">What's the cheapest garage floor option that actually works?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700">A two-part water-based epoxy kit from Rust-Oleum, KILZ, or a comparable brand runs $120–$180 for a one-car garage and provides genuine, lasting protection when properly applied. Below this price point — single-component floor paints, acrylic sealers, or bargain epoxy products — the protection is minimal and typical service life is 1–3 years before peeling or fading. The $120–$180 water-based two-part kit is the minimum investment for real epoxy performance. If you can stretch to $200–$280 with the addition of a separate topcoat, you'll add 2–4 years to the service life and get meaningfully better scratch resistance.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">When is it worth paying for professional installation?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Professional installation earns its premium in four specific situations. First, when your floor has active moisture vapor transmission that requires specialized vapor barrier primers not available to consumers. Second, when your floor has had multiple previous coating failures indicating a substrate problem requiring professional diagnosis. Third, when you want a full polyaspartic system with a 15–25 year warranty — these require spray application equipment that produces a more uniform film thickness than roller application can achieve. Fourth, when your garage is a commercial, showroom, or business space where a coating failure would be significantly disruptive and costly. For a standard residential utility garage on sound concrete, the DIY option delivers excellent results at a fraction of the price.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

