        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            Common Epoxy Cost Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Epoxy quotes vary by a factor of five, and the spread is almost entirely about preparation and coating thickness rather than margin.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing Quotes With Different Preparation</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A bid based on acid etching and a bid based on diamond grinding are not the same job, and grinding is what determines whether the floor lasts. Ask every contractor how they will profile the slab, and compare bids that specify grinding against each other.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not Asking About Total Coating Thickness</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The difference between a thin roll-on system and a full-build flake floor is several times the material, and both get described as "epoxy". Ask for mils of finished thickness and how many coats. That number explains most of the price gap.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating a DIY Kit as Equivalent to a Professional Floor</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A big-box kit costs a few hundred dollars and a professional installation several thousand, and they are genuinely different products in thickness, chemistry, and prep. The kit can be a good value on a light-use garage; it is not a cheaper version of the same thing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving Out Repair and Moisture Mitigation</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Cracks, spalling, and vapour transmission all have to be dealt with before coating, and they are often quoted separately or discovered after the grinder starts. Ask what happens — and what it costs — if the slab tests wet, before you sign.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Epoxy garage floor costs</strong> divide into three tiers: a DIY kit at a few hundred dollars, a professionally installed thin-build coating in the low thousands, and a full-thickness flake or polyaspartic system above that. All three get called epoxy, and they perform very differently.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
            When comparing quotes, the questions that matter are how the slab will be profiled, how many mils of coating go down, and what happens if a moisture test comes back wet. Those three answers explain nearly all of the price difference — far more than the brand of resin does.
          </p>
